<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Andronikos III Palaiologos</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1297">1297</a>–<a href="/years/1341">1341</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Andronicus III Palaeologus · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palaiologos-dynasty">Palaiologos dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Andronikos III Palaiologos was Byzantine emperor from 1328 to 1341, after deposing his grandfather Andronikos II in a long civil war. With his chief minister John Kantakouzenos he recovered Chios from the Genoese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaccaria">Zaccaria</a> in 1329 and annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaly">Thessaly</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a>, but lost most of Byzantine <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>, who defeated him at Pelekanos in 1329.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/andronikos-iii-palaiologos.jpg" alt="Andronikos III Palaiologos" width="320" height="482" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: unknown Byzantine period art. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Andronikos_III_Palaiologos.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Andronikos was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> on 25 March 1297, the son of the co-emperor Michael IX Palaiologos and Rita of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>. In 1320 he accidentally caused the death of his brother Manuel, and his father died in grief soon afterwards. His behaviour and that of his circle of young aristocrats opened a rift with his grandfather, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andronikos-ii-palaiologos">Andronikos II Palaiologos</a>, who disowned him. Andronikos fled, rallied supporters in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a> and began to reign as a rival emperor in 1321. Intermittent civil war followed until 1328, when he became sole emperor on 24 May and his grandfather retired to a monastery.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>His reign saw the last failed attempts to hold back the Ottoman Turks in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bithynia">Bithynia</a>. Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orhan">Orhan</a> defeated a relief attempt for besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaea">Nicaea</a> at the Battle of Pelekanon in June 1329, took Nicaea in 1331 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicomedia">Nicomedia</a> in 1337, and only <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a> and a few ports remained Byzantine in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>. In the Balkans Tsar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-alexander-of-bulgaria">Ivan Alexander of Bulgaria</a> defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> at Rusokastro on 18 July 1332, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> gained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ohrid">Ohrid</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prilep">Prilep</a> and Strumitsa by the peace of 26 August 1334.</p><p>There were also successes. A fleet deposed the Genoese ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martino-zaccaria">Martino Zaccaria</a> and regained Chios in 1329, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phocaea">Phocaea</a> returned in 1334, and Byzantine control was extended over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaly">Thessaly</a> and, from 1337, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a>. John Kantakouzenos, as megas domestikos, held effective administrative authority, while the emperor enjoyed hunting and war. Andronikos also created a panel of four judges, the Universal Justices of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Andronikos III died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> on 15 June 1341, aged 44, possibly of chronic malaria. His son John V Palaiologos was only nine and needed a regent. Within months a dispute over the regency and the position of John Kantakouzenos led to a destructive civil war, which left the empire unable to prevent the rise of the Serbian Empire or the Ottoman entry into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. Historians generally do not blame his leadership for the later decline, and hold that under him the empire came closest to regaining power in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fourth-crusade">Fourth Crusade</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Andronikos III Palaiologos” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1333 Dušan attacked the Byzantine Empire of <b>Andronikos III Palaiologos</b> with the help of the Byzantine general Syrgian, taking Ohrid, Prilep and Kastoria.</blockquote><a href="/empire/serbian-empire">Serbian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1329 the Chiots rose against him with the support of <b>Andronikos III</b>, who landed with a fleet; Martino was captured and Chios returned to Byzantium.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-chios">Lordship of Chios</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.01,28.98&amp;year=1325" aria-label="Constantinople on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Andronikos III Palaiologos"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4934 4064h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4934 4064h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.01,28.98&amp;year=1325">What was here in 1325? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
